From 1c624c6552e90e7cf8f2caff0fca054dd73597c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 16:18:41 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui: Enter does nothing in the composer while a question
 is pending

While the agent waits on an answer to a question it asked -- the
AskUserQuestion card of 0005/0006/0027 -- Enter in the composer sent a
message. The keystroke was usually meant for the card, so the message
went out over an open question, which the agent then had to reconcile
with an answer it never got. Now Enter and the send chords
(ctrl-enter, cmd-enter on macOS, and the modifier-send chord when
use_modifier_to_send is on) do nothing in the composer until the
question is answered, declined or cancelled. The operator answers on
the card -- Tab to its field or a button, Enter there -- or with the
mouse.

BY CHOICE, the Send button still sends while the question is open: the
block lives in the key binding, not in ThreadView::send, which the
button's click listener reaches as well. A click on Send is never
accidental, and keeping it is the operator's decision of 2026-10-05.
ctrl-shift-enter (SendImmediately) is left bound for the same reason --
it is a deliberate chord, not a stray Enter.

How it works:

- MessageEditorAddon puts `question_pending` in the composer's key
  context, read from a Cell shared with MessageEditor
  (set_question_pending).
- ThreadView sets it on every render from
  Conversation::has_pending_elicitation -- the predicate 0030's sidebar
  status reads, so the composer stops sending exactly while the sidebar
  says "Waiting for Your Answer". Reading it per render instead of on
  the elicitation events means every way a question ends clears the
  flag without a path of its own.
- The Linux, macOS and Windows default keymaps bind enter and the send chord in
  `AcpThread > Editor && question_pending` to
  agent::IgnoreSendWhileQuestionPending, a no-op the composer handles.
  Not `null`: Enter carries key_char "\n", and a keystroke no handled
  action consumes falls through to the editor's text input, so an
  unbound Enter would still insert a newline. The action is hidden from
  the command palette -- there is nothing to run from there.
- vim.json gets the same block for `MessageEditor > Editor && VimControl`:
  vim binds Enter to agent::Chat in normal mode, in a keymap loaded after
  the default one, so it would otherwise outrank the block above.

The card's own Enter is untouched: its `Elicitation` context is only
on the dispatch path while the card has focus, and the composer's
context only while the composer does.

Tests (conversation_view::tests, all named *question_pending*): Enter
and ctrl-enter send nothing and insert no newline while the question
is open, and Enter sends again once it is cancelled; the same with
use_modifier_to_send; Enter in vim's normal mode; send() -- the Send
button's path -- still sends;
Tab then Enter from the card's field answers the question.

Story 017. Reads 0027 (the pinned card) and 0030 (the pending state).

Tests added 2026-10-09 (0.2.2_p20261009). A URL elicitation -- open a link, finish in the
browser -- blocks Enter like a form question, pinned on purpose: the reason above
holds for it too, even though it has no card and is drawn inline. Whether a URL
question should block at all is the operator's call; this keeps today's behaviour
from changing unnoticed. And 0005's multi-line field: Enter in the card is
menu::Confirm, so Shift-Enter is the only newline -- it must insert one and leave
the question pending, and Enter still answers. Here rather than in 0005 because it
uses this patch's composer setup.
---

